Z||000003||00lE||11-03-2001||04:47 PM||||Probably better recheck that math there guy    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The 89-90 Turbo Grand Prix used a similar sized turbo running at 8-10 lbs of boost, and it seemed to work just fine.  The mits. tubos were designed to deliver 10-12 lbs of boost on a VERY effecient engine operating at a MUCH higher peak rpm than that of a stock Fiero 2.8.<BR>Horsepower specs for the NA version of the mits. 2.0L were also pretty close to that of the stock Fiero 2.8.<P>For a system on a Fiero with only 8 lbs max boost at its limited redline, these turbos would be a very acceptable choice, and lag would be near non-existant.<P>There are some nice java calculators that should prove useful for those designing their own turbo systems here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.turbofast.com.au/turbokits.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.turbofast.com.au/turbokits.html</A> <P>The only real problems with using the mits. turbo lies in its vertical configuration, which would present some interesting installation challenges.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 11-03-2001).]||||reg||1
